@[user-setup step]
To set up users, chroot into the target system as for the bootloader step:
  @[enchantment chroot]

Set a password for the root user (pick a secure one, but not one you'll forget,
and bear in mind that it can be bypassed with a screwdriver):
  @[passwd root]

@[Recommended]: add at least one regular user for daily use; the "wheel" group
is for users allowed to become root (with sudo, doas or su):
  @[useradd -m -g users -G wheel mbolton]
  @[passwd mbolton]
Replace @[mbolton] with whatever login you want (its home is /home/mbolton).
An existing user can be added to wheel with @[usermod -aG wheel mbolton].

To let wheel members run commands as root with sudo (select the sudo cache in
the spell-caches step, or @[cast sudo] later), run @[visudo] and remove the
"# " in front of this line (visudo checks the file before saving it):
  %wheel ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL
Or doas, a smaller alternative (@[cast opendoas] on the installed system):
put "permit persist :wheel" into /etc/doas.conf.

Finally, exit the chroot to return to the installer:
  @[exit]

Type @[next] to continue to the next step.
